Given a number x and two positions (from the right side) in the binary representation of x, write a function that swaps n bits at given two positions and returns the result. It is also given that the two sets of bits do not overlap.

Input:
x = 47 (00101111)
p1 = 1 (Start from the second bit from the right side)	00101111
p2 = 5 (Start from the 6th bit from the right side)	00101111
n = 3 (No of bits to be swapped)					11100011
Output:
227 (11100011)
The 3 bits starting from the second bit (from the right side) are 
swapped with 3 bits starting from 6th position (from the right side)

1) Move all bits of the first set to the rightmost side
   set1 =  (x >> p1) & ((1U << n) - 1)
Here the expression (1U << n) - 1 gives a number that contains the last n bits set and other bits as 0. We do & with this expression so that bits other than the last n bits become 0.
2) Move all bits of second set to rightmost side
   set2 =  (x >> p2) & ((1U << n) - 1)
3) XOR the two sets of bits
   xor = (set1 ^ set2) 
4) Put the xor bits back to their original positions. 
   xor = (xor << p1) | (xor << p2)
5) Finally, XOR the xor with original number so 
   that the two sets are swapped.
   result = x ^ xor


unsigned int swapNBits(unsigned int n, int pos1, int pos2, int num_bits)
{
    unsigned int mask;
    unsigned int bits1, bits2;
 
    mask = (1U << num_bits) - 1;
 
    bits1 = (n >> pos1) & mask;
    bits2 = (n >> pos2) & mask;
 
    if (bits1 != bits2) {
        n ^= (bits1 ^ bits2) << pos1;
        n ^= (bits1 ^ bits2) << pos2;
    }
 
    return n;
}

unsigned int swapNBits(unsigned int n, int pos1, int pos2, int num_bits)
{
    unsigned int mask;
    unsigned int bits1, bits2;
    unsigned int diff;

    // Create mask for num_bits
    mask = (1U << num_bits) - 1;

    // Extract bits
    bits1 = (n >> pos1) & mask;
    bits2 = (n >> pos2) & mask;

    // Find different bits
    diff = bits1 ^ bits2;

    // Swap the bits
    n ^= diff << pos1;
    n ^= diff << pos2;

    return n;
}

==========================================================
this implementation is correct for swapping num_bits bits at two positions.

What your code does
mask = (1U << num_bits) - 1;

For example, if num_bits = 3:

1U << 3 = 1000
1000 - 1 = 0111

So mask = 0x7, which extracts 3 bits.

Then:

bits1 = (n >> pos1) & mask;
bits2 = (n >> pos2) & mask;

This extracts num_bits starting at pos1 and pos2.

The key operation is:

n ^= (bits1 ^ bits2) << pos1;
n ^= (bits1 ^ bits2) << pos2;

It effectively swaps the two bit groups.

Example

Suppose:

n       = 0b11010110
pos1    = 1
pos2    = 5
num_bits = 2

The groups are:

n = 1 1 0 1 0 1 1 0
          ↑     ↑

At pos1 = 1, 2 bits = 11

At pos2 = 5, 2 bits = 10

After swapping:

Before: 11010110
After : 11100110

So the 2-bit group at position 1 and the 2-bit group at position 5 are exchanged.

